{"id":97,"text":"During a long and bitter winter, a village ran short of food. Families shared what little they had, but the snow lay so deep that nobody could travel to the market.\n\nIn one cottage lived an old widow with a single apple left in her pantry. She looked at it for a long time. Her stomach ached with hunger, but she thought of her neighbour, a young mother with three small children, who had almost nothing.\n\nQuietly, she wrapped the apple in a cloth and left it on the mother’s doorstep before dawn.\n\nThe young mother woke up and found the gift, and her eyes filled with tears. But she, too, remembered an elderly man down the road who lived alone and hadn’t been seen for days. She cut the apple into thin slices, kept one for each child and took the remaining pieces to the old man.\n\nThe old man, deeply moved, remembered that his sick sister lived at the end of the village. He shared his slice with her and gave the rest to a passing shepherd.\n\nBy evening, the shepherd returned to the widow’s cottage, seeking shelter from the storm. “Somebody left me a piece of apple today,” he said. “It was the best thing I’ve ever tasted. Please come and eat with me. I have a sheep, and I’d like to share it.”\n\nBecause of one small act, the whole village had food that night.\n\nMoral: Generosity spreads like warmth, and one small gift can feed many.","tr":null,"gl":{"during":"davomida","a":"bir (noaniq artikl)","long":"uzoq, uzun","and":"va","bitter":"achchiq","winter":"qish","village":"qishloq","ran":"yugurdi","short":"qisqa","of":"-ning; -dan","food":"ovqat","families":"oilalar","shared":"baham ko'rdi","what":"nima","little":"kichkina; oz","they":"ular","had":"bor edi; -gan edi","but":"lekin, ammo","the":"(aniq artikl)","snow":"qor","lay":"joylashgan edi, yotardi","so":"shuning uchun; shunday","deep":"chuqur","that":"u, o'sha; -ki","nobody":"hech kim","could":"qila olardi","travel":"sayohat qilmoq","to":"-ga; uchun","market":"bozor","in":"ichida, -da","one":"bir,